# Configuration for saasumcp. Copy to .env and set what you need.
#
# A commented-out setting shows the DEFAULT it takes when left unset. A BLANK value means there is
# no literal default to show; the comment above it says what happens when it is left unset.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES. It is looked for beside the binary, then at SAASUMCP_ENV, then in the
# project directory, then at /etc/saasumcp/.env. SAASUMCP_ENV names the file itself and has to be
# set in the environment the MCP client starts the server with. `saasumcp app:config` prints the
# file that was actually read.
#
# THIS FILE HOLDS SAASU PASSWORDS. Make it 0600, or root-owned and 0640 for a group of the user
# that runs the server; `saasumcp app:validate` fails anything wider.

# The state directory: cached Saasu tokens, the shared throttle, each file's request count and the
# lists kept for a day. Created 0700. An installed binary MUST set this absolutely: by default it
# follows the working directory, which an MCP client chooses, and each directory would then keep
# its own budget.
SAASUMCP_STATE_PATH=

# The file inventory. files.toml.example is the template.
#SAASUMCP_FILES_PATH=<state directory>/files.toml

# Requests the server may send one Saasu file in any 24 hours, unless the file's entry in the
# inventory sets daily_budget. Keep everything using a file well under its Saasu quota.
#SAASUMCP_DAILY_BUDGET=500

# One login per file in the inventory, by its key uppercased. A login of its own, without
# two-factor authentication, and not one another integration uses.
#SAASUMCP_ACME_USERNAME=
#SAASUMCP_ACME_PASSWORD=

# A second timezone, for reference in answers. Transaction dates are never converted.
#APP_TIMEZONE=UTC

# Seconds a request to Saasu may take, and to wait for the connection.
#SAASU_TIMEOUT=30
#SAASU_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5
